% ============================================================================
% tooth-part-property — a descriptive PROPERTY a source states for a tooth
% part, decoded from clauses already sitting unused inside `tooth-parts.adj`'s
% own already-quoted MedlinePlus/StatPearls source sentences
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `tooth-parts.adj`
% (`tooth_part_role(part, role)`, dentin → beneath_enamel,
% cementum → covers_roots): that table maps each part to WHERE it sits or
% what it IS, but two of that table's own already-quoted spans ALSO state a
% descriptive PROPERTY of the part -- a fact the part/role schema had no room
% for:
%
%     "Just beneath the enamel is dentin, a substance harder than bone."
%     "Cementoblasts form the cementum (calcified material covering the
%      roots of teeth)"
%
% New `tooth_part_property(part, property)` table decodes those spans as
% their own rows: dentin → harder_than_bone, cementum → calcified_material.
% "Property" (a descriptive characteristic of the material itself) is
% categorically distinct from the parent table's "role" (where the part sits
% or what it does) -- dentin's role is beneath_enamel, but its property
% (harder than bone) is a separate fact the role column has no room for.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? tooth_part_property(dentin, $Property)   % harder_than_bone
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `tooth_part_property(part, property)` carrying the citation, so the lookup
% above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns the property
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% anything that is not one of these two parts (it never invents a property).
% The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind a property and recall the part it
% describes:
%
%     ? tooth_part_property($Part, calcified_material)   % cementum
%
% A recall on `enamel` -- a real, already-tabled tooth part in
% `tooth-parts.adj`, but whose own quoted span ("The outer surface of the
% crown is made of enamel.") states only its location, never a descriptive
% property -- correctly abstains rather than inventing one for it. Likewise
% `crown`, `pulp`, and `root` are deliberately left unrowed: their own quoted
% spans never supply a second, descriptive property beyond the role itself.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME two sentences already
% quoted inside `tooth-parts.adj`'s own header, from the SAME MedlinePlus and
% StatPearls pages that table already cites -- no new WebFetch, only a
% different schema decoding a different clause of each already-verified
% quote. `trust authoritative` -- the same tier the parent table already
% carries (MedlinePlus and StatPearls are both primary U.S. government
% NIH/NLM resources).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table tooth_part_property {
    columns part, property

    row (dentin, harder_than_bone)
    row (cementum, calcified_material)

    source "Just beneath the enamel is dentin, a substance harder than bone."
    locator "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002214.htm"
    trust authoritative
}
